<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 15px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # Shapes Around Us – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 1st Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.3)</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Shapes Around Us</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I See a Rectangle.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Speaking: Productive: Name common classroom objects and familiar people. Interactive: Engage with very simple classroom commands.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Speaking: Can identify and name common shapes and colors in daily classroom routines with teacher guidance (e.g., "This is a circle." "It is blue."). Can use simple greetings and farewells in a storytelling activity involving shapes and colors (e.g., "Hello, red circle!" "Goodbye, blue square!"). Can point to and name familiar classroom shapes and colors during activities with teacher prompts.</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; color: #1a5276; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher leads a "Shape Song and Dance." Students sing a simple song about shapes (e.g., "A circle is round, a square has four sides") while performing TPR actions for each shape. Teacher uses large shape flashcards or realia (e.g., a round clock, a square book) and asks students to point as they sing and repeat the shape names. This activates prior knowledge of shapes and colors from previous lessons.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard or classroom object and explicitly models the target language: "Look! This is a rectangle." "It is a big rectangle." The teacher then models the pronunciation of key words, emphasizing the /s/ sound in "square" and the /r/ sound in "rectangle," slowly and clearly. The teacher also models simple WH-questions: "What shape is it?"</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding with simple, concrete questions.</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Holding a circle flashcard) "Is this a square?" (Students respond: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Pointing to a rectangle) "Can you say 'rectangle'?" (Students repeat chorally and individually: "Rectangle!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Shapes Around Us")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short, illustrated story or picture book titled "My Day with Shapes." The story features a child pointing out different shapes in their environment (e.g., "I see a round clock. I see a square window. I see a rectangle door."). This material reviews previously introduced vocabulary and provides context for the speaking activities.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Identifying shapes: Recognize and name shapes like squares and circles during classroom activities. Describing shapes: Use simple phrases to talk about shapes (e.g., "This is a rectangle.").</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher reads the story aloud, pointing to each shape as it is mentioned. Students point to the corresponding shapes on their own small pictures or in the classroom.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher asks simple questions: "What shape is the window?" (Students respond: "Square!") "Is the clock round or square?" (Students respond: "Round!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Grammar:</b> Shape + noun ("a square table"), WH-questions ("What shape is it?"), Present simple ("That is a circle.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Vocabulary:</b> square, rectangle, circle, triangle, oval, diamond, star; it, see, look, draw, find, be (is); round, square, tall, long, short, big, small; this, that.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pronunciation:</b> /s/ in "square" and /r/ in "rectangle"; phonemic awareness of initial sounds in shape names.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Pragmatic:</b> Identifying and describing shapes with simple phrases.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Sociolinguistic:</b> Describing characteristics of objects around using shapes and colors.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. <b>Controlled Practice – "Say and Point":</b> The teacher holds up large flashcards of various shapes one by one. Students chorally repeat the shape name after the teacher (e.g., "Circle!", "Rectangle!"). Then, the teacher says a shape name (e.g., "Find the square!"), and students point to the correct flashcard or a real object in the classroom that matches the shape. The teacher reinforces pronunciation of /s/ and /r/.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. <b>Semi-Controlled Practice – "What Shape Is It?":</b> The teacher uses shape flashcards or classroom objects. The teacher asks, "What shape is this?" pointing to an object. Students respond in complete, simple sentences, first chorally, then individually: "It is a [shape]." or "This is a [shape]." The teacher introduces adjectives: "It is a big square." "It is a long rectangle." and has students repeat.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. <b>Pair Interaction – "Shape Match and Talk":</b> Students work in pairs. Each pair receives a set of small shape flashcards. Student A picks a card, shows it to Student B, and asks, "What shape is it?" Student B responds, "It is a [shape]." or "It is a [color] [shape]." Students switch roles after every two turns. The teacher monitors closely, providing immediate feedback on pronunciation and sentence structure.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Shapes Around Us" Scavenger Hunt and Show & Tell.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. The teacher divides the class into small groups of 3-4 students. Each group receives a checklist with common shapes (e.g., square, rectangle, circle, triangle).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Students move around the classroom (or a designated area) and find objects that match the shapes on their list. When they find an object, they practice saying, "I see a [shape] [object]." (e.g., "I see a rectangle table.") or "That is a [color] [shape]." (e.g., "That is a red square."). The teacher encourages the use of demonstratives "this" and "that".</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3. After finding 2-3 objects, groups return to their seats. Each group then selects one or two objects they found and presents them to the class in a mini "Show & Tell." One student holds the object and says, "This is a [shape] [object]." Another student in the group can add a color or size, "It is a big red rectangle." The teacher prompts with "What shape is it?" or "What color is it?" to encourage interaction.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> "Shape Identification Challenge." The teacher holds up various real classroom objects or large flashcards one at a time. Each student is called upon individually to name the shape and use a simple descriptive phrase. For example, the teacher holds a book, and the student says, "It is a rectangle." or "This is a blue rectangle."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Observation Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Intelligible pronunciation of shape names (e.g., clear /s/ in "square," clear /r/ in "rectangle").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (names at least three shapes: square, rectangle, circle).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Uses simple phrases like "This is a..." or "It is a..." correctly.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds to teacher's prompts and participates in group activities.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students are asked two simple questions:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Can you name three shapes?" (Students show thumbs up for yes, thumbs down for no, or orally respond).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is your favorite shape?" (Students say the name of their favorite shape).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0; color: #1a5276;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new shapes can you say today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to talk about shapes with your friend?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> The "Shape Scavenger Hunt" worked very well for engaging students and encouraging oral production in a fun, active way. The modeling of /s/ and /r/ was effective, but some students still need more practice with the "rectangle" sound. For next time, I need to ensure all pairs have clear instructions and enough time for their "Show & Tell." More visual prompts or sentence starters could scaffold weaker speakers. I will prepare additional flashcards for describing object sizes (big/small, long/short) more explicitly.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson focused on speaking and identifying shapes. For Lesson 4, we will transition to writing by learning to trace and write shape names and simple sentences about shapes we see, building on the vocabulary and phrases practiced today. Students will use the phrases "This is a..." and "It is a..." to describe shapes in writing.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>